course: dictionary
lesson: 10391
title: Triage: sorting by need
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are new to us, but the words around them can help. Triage is a word you may hear in hospitals. «At A&E, the hospital's emergency department, a triage nurse checks everyone on arrival and the sickest go first.» A&E is the place people go in an emergency, so triage must be about who gets help first.
explain: Look at the clue «the sickest go first». Triage means deciding the order people are treated, so the most urgent cases come first. Now try the swap test: "a nurse sorts people by how urgently they need help" fits the sentence, so your meaning works.
explain: The sentence also says «a long wait means your injury is not the most dangerous one there.» A long wait does not mean you are being ignored. It means others need help more urgently. Triage is fair because it is about need, not about who arrived first.
explain: Triage is also used outside hospitals. A teacher or a parent may triage a pile of jobs, doing the most important ones first. As a verb, to triage means to sort in this way. When you are unsure, check a dictionary.
fact: Triage means deciding the order in which people are treated, based on who needs help most urgently.
fact: A triage nurse checks people on arrival at A&E, the hospital's emergency department.
fact: In everyday life, triage means sorting any jobs or problems so the most urgent ones come first.
fact: As a verb, triage means to sort patients or jobs this way, as in «They triaged the jobs.»
fact: Triage is used in hospitals, first aid and after disasters, and it is fair because it is about need.
exercise: «At A&E, a triage nurse checks everyone on arrival.» What does triage mean in this sentence? | Sending everyone home without seeing them | *Sorting people into order of who needs help most urgently | Painting the walls of the waiting room | Counting how many people came in today
exercise: Why might a badly hurt person be seen before someone who came in earlier with a small cut? | *Because triage puts people in order of need, not arrival time | Because triage puts people in order of need | Because they arrived by car | Because the nurse likes them more
exercise: Your teacher has a pile of jobs and sorts them so the most urgent are done first. Which word fits best? | Curt | Glum | Ancient | *Triage
test: In triage, who is treated first? | *The people who need help most urgently | The people who are tallest | The people with the smallest cuts | The people who arrived first, always
test: Which of these is a verb form of triage? | triage system | triage nurse | in triage | *triaged
exam: A nurse says «Mia, you are in triage.» Mia is sitting and waiting. What does this most likely mean? | Mia has already been sent home | Mia has to leave the building right now | *Mia is being sorted by how urgently she needs help | Mia is the nurse's sister
